<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        THE MARKS A ROW WEARS - the coloured shapes that sit beside three of its words.

        SPLIT OUT OF Cells.xaml ON 2026-08-25 BECAUSE THE MARKUP RATCHET ASKED, and the sentence
        this breaks is written here rather than left to rot. Cells.xaml claimed, from 2026-08-12,
        that "everything about a cell is on one side of that seam" - it is not any more, and the
        claim has been rewritten on both sides. What could not be done was the alternative: that
        file stood exactly on the ceiling, `A11` needs a badge beside the name and a template to
        put it in, and shortening a note to make room is recorded twice in this project as a
        failure rather than a saving.

        THE SEAM IS A SUBJECT AND IT PASSES THE SAME TEST EVERY OTHER HALF OF THIS THEME HAD TO -
        a name that answers "what is in there" without opening it, and a claim at the head of the
        file that AppearanceGuards can assert. Cells.xaml holds the WORDS in a cell and what a cell
        is made of. This holds the MARKS: their shared geometry and, for each of the three columns
        that carries one, the triggers that colour it.

        MERGED BEFORE Cells.xaml AND THAT ORDER IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. StaticResource resolves
        while a file is being read, so the templates in Cells.xaml that name StatusMark, StartMark
        and AgainstMark can only find them if this dictionary went in first - the same rule that
        puts Values.xaml before every style file and Text.xaml before Controls.xaml. Anywhere else
        that builds this stack - WpfHost in the tests, tools/wpfui-probe, tools/gui-probe/check -
        repeats these lines in this order for the same reason.

        NOTHING HERE IS IMPLICIT. Every style has a key, so nothing in this file applies to a
        control that did not ask for it, and the position of this dictionary in the merge list
        therefore costs nothing today. It is still the order that will be right the day something
        here stops having a key.

        WHY THE MARKS ARE TEMPLATED AT ALL: `docs/11` 3.1 asks for shape AND colour AND label and
        calls any two of the three insufficient. A dot alone is a colour somebody has to have been
        taught, and a word alone is a column that has to be read row by row.
    -->

    <!--
        THE GEOMETRY EVERY MARK SHARES, AND IT CARRIES NO TRIGGER ON PURPOSE - 2026-08-12,
        backlog 166.

        Until today `StartMark` was BasedOn `StatusMark`, which meant it inherited every one of
        that style's triggers - and those read `StatusShape`, the RUN STATE. A running entry hit
        the "running" trigger, which sets Fill and Stroke; the start column's own "disabled"
        trigger then set Stroke and left the Fill alone. So an entry that was running and
        disabled wore a GREEN FILLED DOT in the column about its next start. Measured on the
        pixel before the repair: #6CCB5F, the same green as the status dot two columns left.

        A mark must answer about its own column and nothing else. The way to guarantee that is
        for the shared half to be geometry with no opinion about any field, which is this style,
        and for each mark to carry its own triggers and only its own. `MarkDistinctionGuards`
        holds the rule now - it walks the BasedOn chain, which is exactly what it could not see
        before, because it read a style's own triggers and never its inherited ones.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="MarkShape" TargetType="Ellipse">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginAfterShape}" />
        <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="{StaticResource ShapeOutline}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />
        <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE MARK BESIDE A MEANING, AND FILLED VERSUS HOLLOW IS NOT DECORATION.

        Filled means the entry is doing something - running, or on its way somewhere. Hollow
        means it is not. Colour separates within each pair, so the shape carries the coarse
        answer and the colour the fine one. WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.1 says colour may not be the only
        visual means of conveying information, and the word beside the mark is the third
        channel and always present.

        The trigger compares against a CODE from the view model, never against the word. The
        word is translated; the code is not, and a trigger matching on "Running" would quietly
        stop matching the day a second language file appears - the row would just lose its
        colour, with nothing anywhere saying why. This project has had four of those.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="StatusMark" TargetType="Ellipse" BasedOn="{StaticResource MarkShape}">
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.running">
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRunning}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRunning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.transit">
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.stopped">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.paused">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <!--
                Explicit rather than left to the style's default, which drew it identically to a
                stopped entry. Backlog 149 - the mark now differs in shape, not in a shade of grey
                nobody can separate.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.unknown">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeDashArray" Value="{StaticResource ShapeUnreadableOutline}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The start type's mark, and it is Hidden rather than Collapsed for the ordinary case.
        Collapsed would give the space back and every word in the column would sit at a
        different place depending on whether its entry had something to report, which is a
        ragged column pretending to be information.

        Waiting on a trigger deliberately gets NO mark. It is not a fault - it is why four of
        the ten entries that read as "automatic and not running" on a real machine were fine.
        It is said in words, next to the type, where it explains rather than accuses.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="StartMark" TargetType="Ellipse" BasedOn="{StaticResource MarkShape}">
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Hidden" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <!--
                Each mark says what it means, backlog 149. Colour was the only carrier until
                2026-08-10 and docs/11 forbids exactly that - the owner reported seeing dots that
                were sometimes empty and sometimes red with no way to find out why.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.missing">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.missing}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.disabled">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.disabled}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.unknown">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeDashArray" Value="{StaticResource ShapeUnreadableOutline}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.unknown}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <!--
                THE FOUR START TYPES, 2026-08-17, owner's decision - until then all four were drawn
                as nothing at all, so the mark in this column answered "is something wrong" and
                never "what is this set to".

                RINGS, NOT FILLS, AND THE THREE TRIGGERS ABOVE ARE WHY. A filled mark here means
                the manager cannot carry out the setting - only the missing file earns that, and it
                is declared first so it wins. These four set Stroke and leave Fill alone, which the
                base style has already put at Transparent.

                Their colours are argued in Themes/Colours.xaml, where they are measured: quiet on
                purpose, separated from each other by calculation, and none of them anywhere near
                the green a running service wears two columns to the left. That last one is backlog
                166, which was exactly this picture arriving by accident.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.start.boot">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource StartBoot}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.boot}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.start.system">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource StartSystem}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.system}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.start.automatic">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource StartAutomatic}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.automatic}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.start.manual">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource StartManual}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.manual}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE THIRD MARK, AND IT IS A TRIANGLE RATHER THAN A DOT ON PURPOSE - backlog 165.

        The column answering "is this entry doing the opposite of its start type" carried the word
        Yes or No alone, between two columns that both wear marks, and `docs/11` 3.1 calls a word
        without a shape insufficient wherever something means something.

        WHY NOT A THIRD DOT. A dot in that position reads as one more answer about the running
        state - which is the confusion the start column was repaired for on the same day. This is a
        PERSISTENT disagreement between two settings rather than a state something is passing
        through, so it gets its own shape family and it does not borrow the amber that means "on
        its way somewhere" either.

        WHY THE COLOUR IS BORROWED AND NOT INVENTED. MeaningUntrusted already carries "something
        here is wrong" for a missing file, and every brush in this product has a declared contrast
        threshold that `ContrastGuards` enforces - a new colour would be a new threshold to argue
        rather than a shape to draw. The two marks that share it are never in the same column and
        differ in shape, which is the rule `MarkDistinctionGuards` holds.

        Only a definite YES is marked. An entry doing what its start type says is the ordinary case,
        and marking seven hundred ordinary rows is how a mark stops meaning anything.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="AgainstMark" TargetType="Path">
        <Setter Property="Data" Value="{StaticResource ShapeAgainst}" />
        <Setter Property="Stretch" Value="Uniform" />
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginAfterShape}" />
        <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="{StaticResource ShapeOutline}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Hidden" />
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />
        <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding AgainstShape}" Value="shape.against">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.against.mark.yes}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <!--
                A broken outline for broken knowledge, the same rule the two dots keep - and the
                only shape in any of the three columns that is not a whole one.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding AgainstShape}" Value="shape.unknown">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeDashArray" Value="{StaticResource ShapeUnreadableOutline}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.against.mark.unknown}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
